//! What a generated grammar is compiled against: the runtime phase.
//!
//! A file the `codegen` module writes is a set of types that read text,
//! and reading text needs two things of the crate that ends up holding them: a
//! stream the types may be written over, and the matchers a `<NAME>` in a
//! pattern calls. Both are here, and nothing here knows anything about the DSL —
//! these items are what the *described* language leans on, while a spec is read,
//! analyzed and generated by the build phase, which is a different set of
//! dependencies altogether.
//!
//! The phases are the two features that say so, `serde` being a third and a
//! build-phase one:
//!
//! - `runtime`, this module: what generated code links against, needing only a
//!   character stream of [`parserc`] and the two classes of [`unicode_ident`].
//! - `codegen`: the reader, the analyzer and the generator, needing `quote` and
//!   `proc-macro2` and nothing of this module, since what it takes from here is
//!   only the names this module's items go by once they are written down.
//!
//! A crate that keeps a generated file and never produces one therefore takes
//! this and leaves the defaults off, which is the case the split is there to
//! serve:
//!
//! ```toml
//! [dependencies]
//! unsyn = { version = "0.13", default-features = false, features = ["runtime"] }
//! ```
//!
//! The generator promises to write nothing of this crate into a file beyond the
//! bound of [`GeneratedInput`] and the calls of [`matcher`], which is what makes
//! that promise checkable: the same split holds in the other direction, since
//! [`GeneratedInput`] is error-agnostic and so a generated grammar reports
//! failures in its own error type rather than in the DSL's.

mod input;
pub mod matcher;

pub use input::GeneratedInput;
